'''Yponomeuta''' (i-pon-ọ̄-mū′tä̤), ''n.'' [NL. (Latreille, 1796), prop. ''Hyponomeuta'', < Gr. ὑπονομεύειν, undermine, < ὑπόνομος, going underground, underground, as a noun an underground passage, < ὑπό, under, + νέμειν, drive.] A notable genus of tineid moths, typical of the family ''Yponomeutidæ'', comprising a number of rather large slender-bodied species, usually white or gray, and often with many small black spots. {{smaller|The larvæ live gregariously in a light web, and feed upon the foliage of different plants. About a dozen species are found in Europe and 7 in North America. ''Y. cognatella'' is exceedingly destructive to apple-trees, depriving them of their leaves.}}

'''Yponomeutidæ''' (i-pon-ọ̄-mū′ti-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Stephens, 1829), < ''Yponomeuta'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of tineid moths, based chiefly upon venational characters, but having a recognizable facies. {{smaller|The larvæ have 16 legs, and in general feed like those of the type genus. Those of ''Atemelia'', however, bore into buds and young twigs. Some 14 genera have been placed in this family by Standinger, but the important genus ''Argyresthia'' and its allies are removed to a distinct family, ''Argyresthiidæ'', by Heinemann and others. Also ''Hyponomeutidæ''.}}

